# Sentry

SDK: `@sentry/tanstackstart-react`.

## Local setup

- Set `VITE_SENTRY_DSN` in `.env.local` (project DSN from sentry.io).
- Server init: `instrument.server.mjs` at repo root (loaded via `NODE_OPTIONS='--import ./instrument.server.mjs'` in `npm run dev`; copied to `dist/server/` on `npm run build`).
- Without the DSN, Sentry does not start (see console warning in `instrument.server.mjs`).

Optional full TanStack Start integration (client `src/client.tsx`, router tracing, Vite `sentryTanstackStart` plugin, `src/start.ts` middleware) is not wired yet — follow [Sentry manual setup](https://docs.sentry.io/platforms/javascript/guides/tanstackstart-react/manual-setup/) when adding it.

## Server functions

Place instrumented handlers in `src/modules/<feature>/server/*.ts`. Routes and components call these server functions (or module exports) — do not call adapters, `getDb()`, or D1 from presentation code.

Wrap the **handler body** with `Sentry.startSpan`, not `createServerFn` itself:

```tsx
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/tanstackstart-react'
import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'

export const myServerFn = createServerFn({ method: 'GET' }).handler(async () => {
  return Sentry.startSpan({ name: 'myServerFn' }, async () => {
    // use case / port work here
  })
})
```

Use span names that match the operation (e.g. `'getPublicCatalog'`, `'saveResource'`), not generic names like `'handler'`.

### What to wrap

Wrap handlers that use `createServerFn` and do real work: D1 (via use cases/adapters), other modules, external fetches, or non-trivial server logic.

### What not to wrap

- One-liner passthroughs with no I/O
- Test-only stubs
- Logic that lives only in routes or components (move I/O to a server function in `modules/<feature>/server/` and instrument there)

Better Auth (`src/routes/api/auth/$.ts`) is not a `createServerFn` — do not apply this span pattern to auth routes unless you add Sentry’s TanStack Start middleware separately.
